so what's coming up is this thing called  the transit of venus uh and the reason  why it's a bit of an event is because  they're kind of they're not that  frequent although people may remember  there was one back in 2004 so you might  think well actually they are fairly  regularly but it turns up there are only  two and then there's a gap for almost  100 years and then another two so they  come you basically get them in pairs  every 100 years  and so if you miss this one you're gonna  have a pretty long wait for the next one  so a transit is just where one object  passes in front of another  and in this particular case the transit  of venus is just venus passing between  us and the sun and of course roughly  speaking you know that happens fairly  regularly because of course the various  planets all orbiting around the sun and  they sort of go you know they overtake  one another so once in a while uh  planets will kind of lie more or less  between us and the sun but because  things sort of wobble a little bit up  and down as well most of the time they  actually miss the disc of the sun and so  the rarity of this event is it just  happens to be right across the face of  the sun so it's not like an eclipse of  the sun where you know the moon happens  to have exactly the same angular size as  the sun pretty much so almost exactly  blots it out because venus is much much  smaller so all that will happen is a  tiny little black dot will trail across  the face of the sun this is one of those  cases where you have to put in all the  usual  take great care warning instructions  about this do not look directly at the  sun with the unaided eye do not whatever  happens look through the sun at the sun  through a telescope uh the only right  way of doing this is with the right  equipment which in this case means using  a pair of those glasses which are  actually designed for looking at the sun  and even then be careful because you've  got to check to make sure for example if  you've got one of those pairs of eclipse  glasses and it's got a tiny little  scratch in it that tiny little scratch  the sunlight streaming straight through  there could do serious damage to your  eyes so make sure that there's in date  they haven't expired and it actually  hasn't got any damage to it before you  look at the sun but if you do having put  all those caveats in if you have a pair  of those those sort of mylar glasses  that are designed for looking directly  at the sun you can actually with the  naked eye see the little black dot just  about of venus traveling across the face  of the sun sort of cuts a rather uneven  slice off one edge of the sun uh it  doesn't so sometimes again exactly where  it passes through the sun just depends  on uh on these relative locations of the  earth and venus so there's both i mean i  guess there's both historical science  and current science right the historical  science is that this was actually how we  figured out how big the universe was  bizarrely um so basically  you could figure out how far away things  were  relative to the distance between the  earth and the sun right so there's this  technique called parallax that allows us  to figure out from the little wobbles of  stars as you view them as the earth goes  around the sun how far that star is away  but that only works if you know exactly  how far the earth is from the sun okay  and so the transit of venus actually  gives a method for figuring out exactly  how far the earth is away from the sun  at least in principle and the way this  works is again actually down to parallax  that if you observe the transit of venus  at some point on the earth and you time  exactly when venus disappear you know  when venus starts crossing in front of  the sun and when it leaves and then you  go to a different place on the earth or  actually since you only get the one shot  to do this somebody else has already  gone to a different place at a different  latitude and  does exactly the same measurement  because they're viewing this event from  slightly different directions one higher  up on the earth the other low down on  the earth they'll actually see the  timings of these events slightly  differently and just from that  difference in timing that actually  allows you with them with a little bit  of trigonometry to figure out how far  the sun is away in terms of how far your  two observers were apart and because you  can measure distances on the earth that  then allows you at least in principle a  way of figuring out the distance to the  sun and hence the scale of the entire  universe so historically that's why it  was sort of important nowadays if you  want to figure out the scale of the  solar system there are much easier ways  of doing it you can for example just  fire a radar a pulse of radar at a  planet and wait for it to bounce back  and then the time it takes to travel  there and back tells you exactly how far  away the planet is so nowadays there are  kind of easier ways and much more  accurate ways of doing it but uh in the  sort of the 19th century when these  measurements were first being made  accurately this was the most accurate  way of figuring out the scale of the  solar system and hence then when you  use that to calibrate other measurements  the scale of the whole universe the  trick was to spread your your observers  out as far as you could so captain cook  made observations of this in tahiti  people went to the southern seas  there's lots and lots of stories of  there was some poor guy french guy who  actually went to the southern seas uh to  to observe the transit of venus the  first one was crack clouded out entirely  he knew that there would be another one  in eight years so he then hung around  there for eight years um second one he  actually made some observations but he  made them from a ship and the ship's  deck was heaving around so much he  didn't actually get any good data so  then he finally made his way back to  france and found that he'd been declared  dead in his absence and so there's  some scary stories that go along with  observing this it's a it has to be a  quite accurate measurement you make and  actually it turned out to be not as easy  to do as had been assumed because  there's a strange effect called the  black drop effect which as  as venus first so basically remember the  thing you need to do is measure very  accurately the time when the transit  begins when venus first starts to travel  across in front of the sun and this  weird thing the black drop effect means  that as venus approaches the sun it's  it's little disk if you're viewing it  through a telescope appears to get kind  of extended into a little droplet shape  almost a pear shape and so actually  measuring the price precise moment where  it first starts to disappear  in front of the sun is actually a very  hard thing to do because it's no longer  a nice perfect circular object anymore  and picking the exact moment when it  starts to travel in front of the sun  turns out not to be something you can  measure particularly precisely kind of  intrinsically  this is about the hardest time you could  possibly try to observe venus when it's  up against the the glaring brightness of  the sun you're much better observing  venus kind of at night when it's well  away from the sun but of course what  you're actually observing there that  transit is something that's being used  now to discover planets going around  other stars  and so these transiting effects we're  actually seeing the little dips in light  that occur when other planets travel in  front of of stars um stars other than  the sun  and so in principle this is kind of a  useful calibration point because we  already know a lot about venus to say  well can we make the same kind of  observation during a transit of venus  that we would make of another planet  going around another star using the  telescope you know elsewhere  now of course it's not quite the same  thing because the sun is blazingly  bright and actually the kind of  instrumentation you would use to detect  a transit in front of another star is  completely different  but nonetheless there's things that we  can learn and and there's been this  rather clever idea that someone's come  up with of rather than observing the sun  directly and seeing one of these  transits occurring they're actually  going to point the hubble space  telescope at the moon  and look at the reflected sunlight  because of course the light you see from  the moon is just reflected sunlight but  of course much fainter so you can point  the hubble space telescope at the moon  without doing irreparable damage to it  that you would if you pointed it at the  sun and they're going to try to observe  the transit of venus by pointing the  telescope at the moon and spotting the  little changes in the light from the  moon as venus transits across in front  of the sun this is an incredibly rare  astronomical event and you're an  astronomer  you're not going to get another chance  are you going to go are you going to  look at it it's a bit early in the  morning for me it's actually so from the  uk it's only visible it's the event  starts before sunrise and so as the sun  rises  in the uk the transit will already be  fairly well advanced and it's about an  hour left of the transit and so that's  about between about 4 30 and 5 30 in the  morning which is a bit early for me  there's not gonna be another one for 100  years it's a little black dot traveling  across the front of the sun

